# TEMPLATE — copy to admin-planning-configmap.yaml and fill in real
# content, exactly like k8s/secret.yaml.example -> k8s/secret.yaml. The
# real file is git-ignored (see root .gitignore) specifically so curated
# planning content can never be accidentally committed.
#
# docs/adr/0022-admin-control-center.md Decision 10 (as amended
# 2026-08-07). This carries the four curated planning sources behind the
# admin console's `planning/*` views.
#
# WHY THIS IS APPLIED BY HAND, NEVER BY CI
#
# A CI-built image only ever contains the tracked git tree, and this repo
# is public. All four of these sources are deliberately untracked — the
# 2026-07-26 untracking decision for BACKLOG.md/PROJECT.md, and the
# 2026-08-06 move of ACTION_PLAN.md into docs/internal/. Getting their
# content into a running pod therefore reuses the same out-of-band
# mechanism every credential in secret.yaml already uses: applied by hand,
# independently on each cluster (production AND ubuntu01), never generated
# from tracked source by the public pipeline.
#
# The security-issues list is treated with the same caution as genuinely
# private material even though each individual fact in it already appears
# somewhere in this repo's tracked ADRs. Compiling scattered facts into one
# curated "here is everything currently unfixed" index is itself a new
# risk, independent of whether any single entry was already discoverable.
#
# SHIP CURATED SLICES, NOT WHOLE FILES
#
# A ConfigMap is hard-capped at 1 MiB across all keys, and `kubectl apply`
# stores a second full copy in its own last-applied-configuration
# annotation — so the practical ceiling is roughly half that. The whole
# docs/internal/ACTION_PLAN.md was already 272 KB when this was written and
# grows daily; the backend additionally refuses any single file over 256 KB
# and reports that section unavailable (with a log line naming the file)
# rather than letting the failure land on your `kubectl apply` step at some
# unpredictable future date. `action-plan-next-up.md` should be the "Next
# Up" slice only — which is all the roadmap view renders anyway.
#
# HOW TO APPLY / REFRESH after editing your local curated files:
#
#   kubectl create configmap admin-planning-docs \
#     --namespace skillstreak \
#     --from-file=action-plan-next-up.md=/path/to/your/action-plan-next-up.md \
#     --from-file=project-roadmap.md=/path/to/your/project-roadmap.md \
#     --from-file=backlog.md=/path/to/your/backlog.md \
#     --from-file=security-issues.md=/path/to/your/security-issues.md \
#     --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
#
# The api Deployment mounts this at /srv/admin-planning (read-only). That
# path is deliberately disjoint from any statically-served directory and
# must stay that way — see k8s/api-deployment.yaml's comment and
# backend/src/admin/admin-planning-docs.service.ts for why.
#
# Every key is optional: a cluster without this ConfigMap boots fine and
# simply reports the planning views as unavailable. The filenames below are
# fixed — the backend never takes a filename from a request, so these four
# are the only ones it will ever read.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: admin-planning-docs
  namespace: skillstreak
data:
  # ACTION_PLAN.md's "Next Up" slice only — not the whole file.
  action-plan-next-up.md: |
    ## Next Up
    REPLACE_ME — paste the curated slice here.
  # PROJECT.md's curated open subset.
  project-roadmap.md: |
    REPLACE_ME
  # BACKLOG.md's curated open subset.
  backlog.md: |
    REPLACE_ME
  # The hand-maintained security-issues list. No template content on
  # purpose — this one has never had a tracked counterpart to copy from.
  security-issues.md: |
    REPLACE_ME
